
CI/CD means Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery (or Deployment).

It is an automated procedure used to build, test, and deploy software.

======

Simple CI/CD Procedure
Developer writes code
        ↓
Push code to GitHub
        ↓
CI: Automatically Build + Test
        ↓
If tests pass
        ↓
CD: Automatically Deploy
        ↓
Application goes Live 🚀

=====

1. CI — Continuous Integration

When you change your project code and push it to GitHub:

Write Code
↓
git add .
↓
git commit
↓
git push

A CI tool automatically:

Checks your code
Installs dependencies
Builds the application
Runs tests
Detects errors

Example:

GitHub
   ↓
GitHub Actions
   ↓
Install Python packages
   ↓
Run tests
   ↓
Build successful ✅

===

2. CD — Continuous Delivery/Deployment

After CI succeeds, the application can automatically deploy to a cloud platform:

CI Successful
      ↓
Docker Build
      ↓
Create Container
      ↓
Deploy to Cloud Run
      ↓
Live Application 🌐

===

Example related to your FastAPI + Docker + Cloud Run project
You update FastAPI code
        ↓
Push to GitHub
        ↓
GitHub Actions starts
        ↓
Run tests
        ↓
Build Docker image
        ↓
Push image to Google Cloud
        ↓
Deploy to Cloud Run
        ↓
New version is LIVE 🚀

====

In one sentence

CI/CD is an automated pipeline that takes your code from development → testing → building → deployment.

CI = Check whether the code works.
CD = Automatically release the working code to users.

So, for your projects, a typical flow is:

Python/FastAPI → GitHub → GitHub Actions (CI/CD) → Docker → Google Cloud Run → Live Website.

For this FastAPI workflow, the deployment path becomes concrete when 
GitHub Actions builds the Docker image and deploys it to Cloud Run after tests pass, so each successful push can publish a new version automatically.

